<!--
Rendered into the Epic description as markdown. Delete this comment when
rendering.

Five required headings, in this order: Outcome, Problem, Scope, Out of scope,
Success criteria. A reader scanning a board relies on the same sections being in
the same places, and those five are what the decision "does this work belong in
this epic" actually needs.

Dependencies, Technical notes and Links are optional. Drop an optional heading
entirely when it has nothing in it. A required section with nothing to say gets
an explicit "None known" or "Not yet decided", never a placeholder like TBD and
never an empty heading, which reads as an oversight rather than a decision.

There is no word cap. Give the reader the outcome and the boundary in full, and
cut the padding rather than the content. The prose rules, the banned
punctuation and the placeholder rules are in the issue-writing reference.

Never state how many stories sit under this epic, and never list them. The child
links are the source of truth and they update themselves; a count in the
description is stale the moment a story is added or split.
-->

## Outcome

<What is true after this epic ships that is not true now. One paragraph, in
terms of the people affected rather than the components changed.>

## Problem

<What is wrong today, and what it costs. Evidence rather than assertion where
evidence exists.>

## Scope

<What is in. Bullet the workstreams, not the tickets. The stories under this
epic are where tickets live.>

## Out of scope

<What a reasonable reader would assume is included and is not. This section
prevents more rework than any other.>

## Success criteria

<How anyone can tell this worked, stated so that the answer is yes or no.
Include the measure and where it is read from.>

## Dependencies (optional)

<Teams, systems, or decisions this epic waits on. Name the owner of each, or say
that it is unowned. An unowned dependency is a risk, not a blank.>

## Technical notes (optional)

<Constraints, chosen approach, and anything already ruled out with the reason.>

## Links (optional)

<Specs, designs, prior discussion. Links only, never copied content: copied
content goes stale where it sits, and nobody knows which copy is current.>
